"48_parallelmandel.mst — ParallelMandel: the multi-Smalltalk-worker capstone
 (docs/multi-smalltalk-worker.md M4). The SAME zooming Mandelbrot dive as
 MandelZoom (world/45), but each frame is computed IN PARALLEL by N worker VMs
 — one horizontal band each, every worker its own heap + JIT + core — while
 this, the primary VM, only assembles the bands and blits the frame. With N
 workers the frame computes ~N times faster than the single-VM MandelZoom: the
 parallelism is real, and visible as a faster dive.

 How a frame flows (all async, zero polling — §3.1's router):
   step (frame tick):  round complete? -> present + zoom + kick next round
   kickRound:          send {y0. rows. cR. cI. scale} to each worker; each
                       writes its rows straight into the shared framebuffer
   worker:             ParallelMandel computeBand: params  (the same class,
                       loaded from the same image — pure compute, no pane)
 Replies arrive as WorkerInbox dispatches between frame ticks. Since SM2 a
 reply carries only a row count — the pixels were written into GPU-shared
 memory by the worker itself (docs/shared_screen_memory_design.md), so a band
 no longer crosses the worker boundary as a pickle, is not copied into an
 assembly buffer, and is not blitted.

 Launch from the Demos menu, or a Workspace:  ParallelMandel launch
 (Needs a worker-boot registration — the GUI provides one; a plain CLI run
 without one raises 'cannot spawn' cleanly.)"
"I am the parallel Mandelbrot demo: the same zooming seahorse-valley dive
 as MandelZoom, but every frame is computed in parallel by four worker VMs
 — one horizontal band each, every worker with its own heap, JIT and core
 — while I, on the primary VM, only assemble the bands and blit the frame.
 The dive therefore runs about four times faster than the single-VM
 MandelZoom: the parallelism is real and visible. Launch me from the Demos
 menu, or a Workspace: ParallelMandel launch. My class-side computeBand:
 is the pure-compute half that runs inside each worker via the Worker RPC
 protocol. I need an environment that can spawn workers (the GUI provides
 one); a plain CLI run raises a clean cannot-spawn error."
Object subclass: ParallelMandel [
    | pane workers pendingBands centerR centerI scale zoomStep |

    ParallelMandel class >> launch [
        "Spawn the band workers and start the parallel dive."
        ^self new start
    ]
    ParallelMandel class >> workerCount [ ^4 ]
    ParallelMandel class >> maxIter [ ^150 ]

    "── the worker half: pure compute (runs inside each worker VM) ──"
    ParallelMandel class >> computeBand: params [
        "params = {y0. rows. centerR. centerI. scale}. Rasterize those rows
         STRAIGHT INTO THE SCREEN and answer how many were done.

         This used to build a `rows * 320` ByteArray and hand it back, which
         the primary copied into an assembly buffer and then blitted — the band
         crossed the worker boundary as a pickle, was copied once more into
         `buf`, and a third time into the pane. Since SM2 it crosses nothing:
         `screenMemory` is a process-global publication and a worker is a
         thread in this process, so the buffer this worker writes IS the buffer
         the GPU samples. Four workers rasterizing four bands are four threads
         storing into disjoint rows of one screen.

         Nothing synchronises them, and nothing needs to: the bands are
         disjoint by construction, and the primary only presents once every
         band of the round has replied — so no frame is torn and no worker is
         still writing the buffer being shown.

         The escape-time Double math is Mandelbrot>>escapeAtRe:im: (world/35),
         JIT-compiled in THIS worker's own tier-1 compiler."
        | y0 rows cR cI sc mb stepc rmin imin ci cr n fb stride |
        y0 := params at: 1.
        rows := params at: 2.
        cR := params at: 3.
        cI := params at: 4.
        sc := params at: 5.
        "`basicNew`, not `new`: GamePane class>>new installs a 16-colour
         palette, and a worker has no business re-sending that per band."
        fb := GamePane basicNew screenMemory.
        "No direct pane (headless, or the WKWebView GUI): compute nothing and
         say so, rather than failing."
        fb isNil ifTrue: [ ^0 ].
        stride := GamePane basicNew screenStride.
        mb := Mandelbrot new maxIter: self maxIter.
        stepc := sc / 320.0.
        rmin := cR - (sc / 2.0).
        imin := cI - ((240.0 * stepc) / 2.0).
        y0 to: y0 + rows - 1 do: [ :py |
            ci := imin + (py asDouble * stepc).
            cr := rmin.
            0 to: 319 do: [ :px |
                n := mb escapeAtRe: cr im: ci.
                fb byteAt: (py * stride) + px + 1 put: (n >= self maxIter
                    ifTrue: [ 16 ]
                    ifFalse: [ 17 + (n \\ 239) ]).
                cr := cr + stepc ] ].
        ^rows
    ]

    "── the primary half: spawn, orchestrate, assemble, show ──"
    start [
        pane := GamePane new.
        "Ask for the direct framebuffer BEFORE the palette, so the palette
         entries reach it: the pane is built on this command, and PaletteAt
         only lands on a pane that exists."
        pane openDirect: 320 height: 240.
        self buildPalette.
        centerR := 0.0 - 0.743643887037151.   "the seahorse valley, as world/45"
        centerI := 0.131825904205330.
        scale := 3.5.
        zoomStep := 0.9.
        workers := Array new: self class workerCount.
        1 to: workers size do: [ :k |
            workers at: k put: (Worker spawn:
                'Worker onMessage: [:m | Worker reply: (ParallelMandel computeBand: m payload)]') ].
        self kickRound.
        pane onStep: [ self step ]; onReset: [ self teardownWorkers ]; run.
        ^self
    ]

    teardownWorkers [
        "GamePane's reset hook: this session is ending — however (Escape, the
         close button, a re-launch, an orphaned pane closed on primary-VM
         restart). Without this, `workers` — 4 spawned VMs per launch — was
         never terminated, permanently consuming slots against the fleet's
         16-worker cap (src/runtime/workers.rs) until repeated Demos use
         exhausted it and a later launch's `Worker spawn:` started failing
         mid-`start`, after the window/palette had already appeared."
        workers isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        workers do: [ :w | w isNil ifFalse: [ w terminate ] ].
        workers := nil.
    ]

    buildPalette [
        "Identical to MandelZoom's: 16 = near-black interior, 17..255 a
         cyclic colour band keyed by escape count."
        | k |
        pane paletteAt: 16 r: 8 g: 8 b: 24.
        k := 0.
        [ k < 239 ] whileTrue: [
            pane paletteAt: 17 + k
                 r: (k * 8) \\ 256
                 g: (k * 5) \\ 256
                 b: 64 + ((k * 11) \\ 192).
            k := k + 1 ].
        ^self
    ]

    kickRound [
        "Fan this frame out: one band request per worker, each with a
         continuation that simply counts it in. Fully async — the primary
         goes straight back to sleep; band replies dispatch between ticks."
        | rows |
        rows := 240 // workers size.
        pendingBands := workers size.
        1 to: workers size do: [ :k |
            (workers at: k)
                send: (self paramsForBand: (k - 1) * rows rows: rows)
                onReply: [ :r | self bandArrived: r ] ].
        "The continuation carries no pixels now — just the news that the band
         is on screen."
        ^self
    ]
    paramsForBand: y0 rows: rows [
        | p |
        p := Array new: 5.
        p at: 1 put: y0.
        p at: 2 put: rows.
        p at: 3 put: centerR.
        p at: 4 put: centerI.
        p at: 5 put: scale.
        ^p
    ]
    bandArrived: r [
        "A worker finished its band. There is nothing to collect: the pixels
         were written into the screen itself, so all that arrives is the news
         that they are there."
        pendingBands := pendingBands - 1.
        ^self
    ]

    step [
        "Frame tick: only when EVERY band of the current round has landed do
         we show the frame and kick the next round — the pane never shows a
         torn frame, and a slow band simply lets ticks pass (no busy work).

         That rule now does double duty. It was about not showing a
         half-assembled picture; since SM2 it is also what keeps the workers
         off the buffer being displayed, because presenting rotates which of
         the three buffers is the write target and the next round is kicked
         only afterwards."
        pendingBands = 0 ifTrue: [
            pane present.
            scale := scale * zoomStep.
            scale < 0.00005 ifTrue: [ scale := 3.5 ].
            self kickRound ].
        ^self
    ]
]
